-- title:
--   Fibres of the fine Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation, flat-locally
-- statement:
--   The theorem is stated for the following data.
--
--   **Arithmetic base.** Primes $r$ and $\bar r$ with $\bar r \neq r$, a nonzero $N$ with $r \nmid N$ and $\bar r \nmid N$; a characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O$ which is a discrete valuation ring (`hdvr`), an irreducible $\pi \in \mathcal O$ such that $\mathcal O$ is $(\pi)$-adically complete, $\#(\mathcal O/(\pi)) = r$ and $(r) = (\pi)$ in $\mathcal O$; a characteristic-zero field $K_0$ which is a fraction field of $\mathcal O$. A characteristic-zero domain $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ (written `Onr`), an $\mathcal O$-algebra, together with an $\mathcal O$-algebra automorphism $\mathrm{Fr}$, subject to: $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ is complete for the ideal generated by the image of $\pi$, that ideal is maximal, every element of $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ satisfies a monic polynomial over $\mathcal O$ modulo that ideal (`hOnr_alg`), every monic polynomial of positive degree over $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ has a root modulo that ideal (`hOnr_closed`), and $\mathrm{Fr}(x) \equiv x^r$ modulo that ideal (`hFr`). A homomorphism $v\det \colon \mathrm{GL}_2(K_0) \to \mathbb Z$ (written multiplicatively) characterised by `hvdet`: $v\det(g) = n$ if and only if $\det g = u\,\pi^{n}$ in $K_0$ for some $u \in \mathcal O^{\times}$.
--
--   **Indefinite quaternion moduli data.** Rationals $a, b$ with `hB`: $0 < a$ or $0 < b$, and for every height-one prime $w$ of $\mathbb Z$ the completion $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b] \otimes_{\mathbb Q} \mathbb Q_w$ is a division algebra exactly when $w$ lies over $r$ or over $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda$ in $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a,b]$. A scheme $\mathcal X$ with $f \colon \mathcal X \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and an assignment `pt` sending a ring $S$, a morphism $s \colon \operatorname{Spec} S \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and a fake elliptic curve $E$ of level $N$ over $S$ to an $s$-point of $f$, with `h𝒳` asserting that this is a coarse moduli datum in the sense of `IsCoarseModuli` (invariance under isomorphism, compatibility with base change, bijectivity on points over algebraically closed fields, and the universal property against any other such functorial assignment). An integer $n \geq 3$ with $r \nmid n$, $\bar r \nmid n$ and $\gcd(n,N) = 1$; a scheme $M$ with $f_M \colon M \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and `ptF` on fake elliptic curves with full level-$n$ structure, with `hM` asserting `IsFineModuli` (isomorphism-invariance, base-change compatibility, and bijectivity of `ptF` over every ring $S$). A group $G$, a homomorphism $\rho \colon G \to \operatorname{Aut} M$ and a labelling $\chi \colon G \to \Lambda$ with `hρ` asserting `IsLevelTwistAction`: each $\rho(g)$ lies over the base, $\rho(g)$ realises twisting of the full level structure by $\chi(g)$, and $\chi$ is multiplicative, surjective and injective modulo $n\Lambda$ in the precise senses `label_one`, `label_mul`, `label_surjective`, `label_injective`.
--
--   A morphism $p \colon M \to \mathcal X$ with $p$ over the base ($p \circ \ldots$, formally `hp`: $p$ followed by $f$ equals $f_M$), invariant under $\rho$ (`hρp`), and compatible with the moduli maps (`hp_pt`: `ptF S s u` followed by $p$ is `pt S s u.1`). For each prime $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$ a scheme $\mathcal Y_\ell$ with $g_\ell \colon \mathcal Y_\ell \to \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ and `ptT` on curves with extra $\ell$-level structure, each a coarse moduli datum in the sense of `IsCoarseModuliT` (`h𝒴`); two degeneracy morphisms $d_0(\ell), d_1(\ell) \colon \mathcal Y_\ell \to \mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (`hd₀f`, `hd₁f`) such that $d_0$ forgets the extra level (`hd₀`) and $d_1$ records the $\ell$-isogenous quotient: whenever `FakeEllipticCurve.IsLevelIsogeny ℓ u d` holds, `ptT ℓ S s u` followed by $d_1(\ell)$ is `pt S s d` (`hd₁`). Two endomorphisms $a_r, a_{\bar r}$ of $\mathcal X$ over $\operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O$ (`harf`, `harbarf`) inducing on points the Atkin–Lehner quotients at $r$ and at $\bar r$ respectively (`har`, `harbar`).
--
--   **Definite quaternion data.** Rationals $a_1, b_1$ with `hdef`: $a_1 < 0$, $b_1 < 0$ and $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ is ramified exactly at the primes over $\bar r$; a maximal order $\Lambda_1$, an Eichler order $R_1$ of level $N$ with $R_1 \leq \Lambda_1$; an adelic unit $n_1$ in the Hecke set `primeHeckeSet R₁ r` such that $R_1 \cap n_1 R_1 n_1^{-1}$ (the `meetOrder`) is Eichler of level $Nr$ (`hS₁`); an injective $\mathbb Q$-algebra embedding $\iota_0 \colon \mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1] \to M_2(K_0)$; a height-one prime $v$ of $\mathbb Z$ containing $r$. A subgroup $\tilde\Gamma$ (written `Γt`) of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]^{\times}$ having the same elements as [`CerednikDrinfeld.CosetGraph.awayUnits R₁ v`](def/CerednikDrinfeld_CosetGraphAtPrime.html#L37), i.e. the units that are integral for $R_1$ at every prime $w \neq v$. Families $s(\ell)$ of units of $\mathbb H[\mathbb Q,a_1,b_1]$ and $s_f(\ell)$ of adelic units indexed by primes $\ell \notin \{r,\bar r\}$, subject to `hs`: the component of $s_f(\ell)$ at every prime not containing $r$ is $s(\ell) \otimes 1$, its component at primes containing $r$ is $1$, the product of the diagonal image of the scalar $\ell$ with $s_f(\ell)^{-1}$ lies in `levelHeckeUSet Λ₁ (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` when $\ell \mid N$ and in `primeHeckeSet (meetOrder R₁ n₁) ℓ` otherwise, and $\operatorname{nrd}(s(\ell)) = \ell$; together with $\tilde\Gamma_\ell = \tilde\Gamma \cap s(\ell)\tilde\Gamma s(\ell)^{-1}$ (`hΓtℓ`). A unit $\bar w$ with $\operatorname{nrd}(\bar w) = \bar r$ normalising $\tilde\Gamma$ (`hwbar`). A homomorphism $\theta_t \colon \tilde\Gamma \to G$.
--
--   **The uniformisation map.** For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, a map
--   $$\Theta_f \colon \bigl((\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \mathrm{DeligneDatum}_\pi(B)\bigr) \times G \longrightarrow \{\varphi \colon \operatorname{Spec} B \to M \ \text{over}\ \operatorname{Spec}\mathcal O\},$$
--   where the middle factor is the value at $B$ of the functor `Omega K₀ π` (Deligne data: for every full lattice $M \subset K_0^2$ a $B$-submodule of $B \otimes_{\mathcal O} M$ with invertible quotient, monotone in $M$, equivariant for scalar homotheties, and nondegenerate at every prime of $B$), the first factor is `AlgFunctor.corep Onr` and the third is the constant functor $G$. It is subject to four axioms: `hnat`, naturality in $B$ for $\mathcal O$-algebra maps; `hG`, that $\rho(h)$ carries $\Theta_f(x, gh)$ to $\Theta_f(x,g)$; `hinv`, that $\Theta_f(x', \theta_t(\gamma)g) = \Theta_f(x,g)$ whenever $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ and `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` holds for $\iota_0(\gamma)$, $x$, $x'$ (that is, $x'$ has coefficient map $x$'s composed with $\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det \iota_0(\gamma)}$, and $x'$'s Deligne datum is the pull-back of $x$'s along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$); `het`, a formal étaleness statement: for a surjective $\mathcal O$-algebra map $p \colon B \to B_0$ with $\pi$ nilpotent in both and with the property that any two elements of $\ker p$ have product zero, every $B$-point of $M$ lying over $\Theta_f(x_0)$ comes from a unique lift of $x_0$; and `hgeom`, the description over an algebraically closed field $k$ which is an $\mathcal O$-algebra with $\pi$ nilpotent: for every $\psi \colon \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} k$ every $k$-point of $M$ is of the form $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g)$, and $\Theta_f((\psi,P),g) = \Theta_f((\psi',P'),g')$ holds if and only if there is $\gamma \in \tilde\Gamma$ with $g' = \theta_t(\gamma)g$, with $P'$ the pull-back of $P$ along $\iota_0(\gamma)^{-1}$, and with $\psi'(y) = \psi(\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det\iota_0(\gamma)}y)$ for every $y \in \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ that is fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{v\det \iota_0(z)}$ for all central $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ (those with $z = c\cdot 1$ for some $c \in \mathbb Q$) satisfying $\theta_t(z) = 1$.
--
--   **Conclusion.** For every $\mathcal O$-algebra $B$ in which $\pi$ is nilpotent, all $x_1, x_2 \in (\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}} \to_{\mathcal O} B) \times \mathrm{DeligneDatum}_\pi(B)$ and all $g_1, g_2 \in G$ with $\Theta_f(x_1,g_1) = \Theta_f(x_2,g_2)$, there exist a natural number $k$, a finite family of commutative rings $B'_i$ ($i \in \{1,\dots,k\}$), each an $\mathcal O$-algebra and a $B$-algebra with compatible scalar tower over $\mathcal O$, each flat as a $B$-module, jointly covering $\operatorname{Spec} B$ in the sense that every prime of $B$ is the contraction of some prime of some $B'_i$ (`hcov`), and elements $\gamma_i \in \tilde\Gamma$, such that for every $i$ the following three assertions hold, where $x_1, x_2$ are transported to $B'_i$ along the structure map $B \to B'_i$:
--
--   (i) the Deligne datum of $x_2$ over $B'_i$ is the pull-back of that of $x_1$ over $B'_i$ along $\iota_0(\gamma_i)^{-1}$, in the sense of `DeligneDatum.IsPullback`: for every full lattice $M$, the line of the second datum at $M$ is the preimage of the line of the first datum at $\iota_0(\gamma_i)^{-1}M$ under the induced base-changed isomorphism;
--
--   (ii) for every $y \in \mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$ fixed by $\mathrm{Fr}^{v\det\iota_0(z)}$ for all $z \in \tilde\Gamma$ that are rational scalars ($z = c \cdot 1$ with $c \in \mathbb Q$) and satisfy $\theta_t(z) = 1$, the coefficient map of $x_2$ over $B'_i$ sends $y$ to the value at $y$ of the coefficient map of $x_1$ over $B'_i$ composed with $\mathrm{Fr}^{-v\det\iota_0(\gamma_i)}$ (the `frobTwist` by $-v\det\iota_0(\gamma_i)$);
--
--   (iii) $g_2 = \theta_t(\gamma_i)\,g_1$.
--
--   Thus the relation forced on the coefficient maps is only the one on the subring fixed by the Frobenius powers attached to central elements of $\tilde\Gamma$ in the kernel of $\theta_t$, not the full `OmegaNr.IsTwistedAct` relation on all of $\mathcal O^{\mathrm{nr}}$.
--
--   This is the fibrewise injectivity statement for the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation map $\Theta_f$ of the fine moduli scheme of fake elliptic curves with full level-$n$ structure: two points with the same image become related by a single element of $\tilde\Gamma$ after passing to a finite flat covering family of the base. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.existsUnique_factor_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.QM.IsFineModuli.existsUnique_factor_of_cerednikDrinfeld_uniformization_fine), where, combined with a descent argument, it yields the factorisation of $\Theta_f$ through the quotient by the $\tilde\Gamma$-action.
